#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475c0859-7fcc-5659-abd8-58e1b393c37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316395} {:answer/id #uuid "293dbcb2-7a2d-5e82-9de9-5dbace7662f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316396} {:answer/id #uuid "96b35e10-146b-5f3e-aff3-06c6f77981b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316397} {:answer/id #uuid "669d5ef9-aaf0-5f35-b7b5-80d20c0be133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316398} {:answer/id #uuid "62e81304-d6ca-5bfb-88a4-14d4b95923a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b35e10-146b-5f3e-aff3-06c6f77981b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316397} :answer-field/id #uuid "c88c9f99-3dd9-520b-bbe3-2b38faec9e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225ad7f2-9d35-5125-9718-e80b689027c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103989" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x-4=A(x-4)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4)-4 & =A(4-4)+B(4) \\\\ 4 & =4B \\\\ 1 & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =A(0-4)+B(0) \\\\ -4 & =-4A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29228550-4e97-5904-a7c7-467c5e70f2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316427} {:answer/id #uuid "05c4b379-dcf0-59a4-8582-f3ec5b5191a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316428} {:answer/id #uuid "ef95d2f7-960e-56a1-acdd-2683ff67d708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{8}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316429} {:answer/id #uuid "34a25c5f-0dde-5ad6-bec6-f91e14f37efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316430} {:answer/id #uuid "1291d401-1838-566b-a356-1b81bf63defc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1291d401-1838-566b-a356-1b81bf63defc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316431} :answer-field/id #uuid "38747f9e-0786-5d9e-90d4-91755e935282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf0cd4f5-d657-58c6-bc41-65da13b8c417" :question/math-academy-id "q-75828" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+16x+3=(5x+1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3} & =\\frac{14}{(5x+1)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(5x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n14=A(x+3)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-3+3)+B(5(-3)+1) \\\\ 14 & =-14B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}+3)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{14}{5}A \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}=\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|{|}_{4}^{11} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
